--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertDoesNotThrow` fails as its own class carrying the caught throw's message, takes the pending throw so nothing downstream sees it, and agrees with a hand-written `try` over a table of bodies — `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed` and `rule:testing/runner-is-strict`, `rule:errors/propagation`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The member's whole output on failure is the message of the throw it caught,
// so this case asks what that quoting can and cannot say -- an empty message, a
// throw raised several frames down, one raised by a `Core` member rather than
// by `throw`, and one that is itself an assertion's report. The second half is
// the part a single line cannot show: the pending throw is *taken*
// (`nvs_stdlib::test`'s doc comment at the helper owns why), so the class that
// propagates is this member's own and the next assertion starts clean.

use Core\Test;

final class Body {
    // Several frames, so the message quoted is the throw's own and not the
    // name of whatever the callback called first.
    public static function deep(int $depth): void {
        if ($depth == 0) {
            throw new RuntimeError("the bottom of the stack");
        }
        Body::deep($depth - 1);
    }

    // A `Throwable` may carry no message at all, and then there is nothing
    // after the colon -- the report is still the member's own.
    public static function blank(): void {
        throw new LogicError("");
    }

    // Not a `throw` in source: `rule:types/arithmetic` makes `/ 0` an `ArithmeticError`,
    // and this member cannot tell the two origins apart.
    public static function divide(): void {
        int $zero = 0;
        float $q = 7 / $zero;
        echo $q, "\n";
    }

    // A failed assertion is a throw like any other, so its report is quoted
    // inside this one's -- and the colon in it is not a separator this member
    // parses.
    public static function failing(): void {
        Test::assertTrue(false);
    }

    public static function quiet(): void {
    }

    // A body need not answer `void`: the value comes back and is released,
    // never inspected.
    public static function value(): string {
        return "returned";
    }
}

// 1. The four throw shapes, each quoted verbatim after `$body` threw:.
try {
    Test::assertDoesNotThrow(fn (): void => { Body::deep(3); });
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromDepth) {
    echo $fromDepth->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Test::assertDoesNotThrow(fn (): void => { Body::blank(); });
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromBlank) {
    echo "[", $fromBlank->message, "]\n";
}

try {
    Test::assertDoesNotThrow(fn (): void => { Body::divide(); });
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromArithmetic) {
    echo $fromArithmetic->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Test::assertDoesNotThrow(fn (): void => { Body::failing(); });
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromAssertion) {
    echo $fromAssertion->message, "\n";
}

// 2. The class that propagates is this member's, not the body's. The two
// clauses are written in the order that would bind the body's own class first
// if the throw were being passed through -- `RuntimeError` is what `deep`
// raises, and it does not bind.
try {
    Test::assertDoesNotThrow(fn (): void => { Body::deep(0); });
} catch (RuntimeError $asBody) {
    echo "bound as the body's own class\n";
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $asFailure) {
    echo "bound as Core\\Test\\Failure\n";
}

// 3. And the pending throw is gone: the very next assertion holds rather than
// inheriting it, which is the half of `rule:errors/propagation`'s protocol a report cannot show.
Test::assertDoesNotThrow(fn (): void => { Body::quiet(); });
Test::assertDoesNotThrow(fn (): string => Body::value());
echo "two held after a failure\n";

// 4. The agreement, counted: over a table of bodies the member holds exactly
// where a hand-written `try` around the *same* callback catches nothing. Each
// row is asked both ways, so a member that grew its own idea of which throws
// count fails here while every message above still reads correctly.
final class Ran {
    public static function asserted(callable(): mixed $body): string {
        try {
            Test::assertDoesNotThrow($body);
        } catch (Core\Test\Failure $fromMember) {
            return "threw";
        }
        return "completed";
    }

    public static function byHand(callable(): mixed $body): string {
        try {
            $body();
        } catch (Throwable $fromHand) {
            return "threw";
        }
        return "completed";
    }
}

array<callable(): mixed> $bodies = [
    fn (): void => { Body::quiet(); },
    fn (): string => Body::value(),
    fn (): void => { Body::deep(2); },
    fn (): void => { Body::blank(); },
    fn (): void => { Body::divide(); },
    fn (): void => { Body::failing(); },
];

int $agreed = 0;
int $completed = 0;
foreach ($bodies as callable(): mixed $body) {
    if (Ran::asserted($body) == Ran::byHand($body)) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
    if (Ran::asserted($body) == "completed") {
        $completed = $completed + 1;
    }
}
echo "agreed=", $agreed, "/6 completed=", $completed, "/6\n";

// 5. `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R2's bag sits in front of the report here as everywhere else.
try {
    Test::assertDoesNotThrow(fn (): void => { Body::deep(1); }, {message: "the migration applies"});
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $withMessage) {
    echo $withMessage->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Test::assertDoesNotThrow failed: `$body` threw: the bottom of the stack
[Core\Test::assertDoesNotThrow failed: `$body` threw: ]
Core\Test::assertDoesNotThrow failed: `$body` threw: Division by zero
Core\Test::assertDoesNotThrow failed: `$body` threw: Core\Test::assertTrue failed: `$actual` is false
bound as Core\Test\Failure
two held after a failure
agreed=6/6 completed=2/6
the migration applies: Core\Test::assertDoesNotThrow failed: `$body` threw: the bottom of the stack
